module Stryker
  module Runtime
    # Tells whether the code that is running runs while a file loads, from
    # the call stack, as
    # [The stack check](https://github.com/setdef/stryker_for_ruby/blob/trunk/docs/contributors/design/coverage.md#the-stack-check)
    # specifies.
    #
    # The outermost frame of a file's load is its top-level frame. Its
    # `absolute_path` is the file's path, and its label says how Ruby
    # compiled the file: `<compiled>` for a file the loader's hook compiled
    # with `RubyVM::InstructionSequence.compile`, `<top (required)>` for a
    # file Ruby compiled itself, and `<main>` for one Bootsnap compiled. A
    # class body, a module body, and a block that runs while the file loads
    # all have that frame beneath them. A method that the file defines, run
    # after the load, doesn't.
    #
    # ## Examples
    #
    # With `lib/invoice.rb` holding `TOTAL = Stryker::Runtime::Stack.loading?(Set[__FILE__])`:
    #
    # ```ruby
    # require_relative "lib/invoice"
    # TOTAL
    # # => true
    # Stryker::Runtime::Stack.loading?(Set[File.expand_path("lib/invoice.rb")])
    # # => false
    # ```
    module Stack
      # Whether the stack holds the top-level frame of a file at one of
      # `paths`. It reads the whole stack, however deep, and stops at the
      # first such frame. With no paths, it is false. A frame that has no
      # path, such as one of an `eval`, never matches.
      #
      # Each check costs 1.9 to 3.3 µs at 25 frames, a test body's usual
      # depth, and 22 to 40 µs at 305 frames, under a UTF-8 locale. Under the
      # C locale a path with a byte beyond ASCII makes each check cost 1.3 to
      # 2.3 times as much as a path of ASCII bytes. [Measuring the
      # check](https://github.com/setdef/stryker_for_ruby/blob/trunk/docs/contributors/design/coverage.md#measuring-the-check)
      # gives the script, the commands, and the Rubies.
      #
      # - `paths`: Set of String, the files' paths as
      #   `Stryker::Runtime::PathKey.of_file` gives them, called with the same
      #   `separator`.
      # - `separator`: String or nil, the system's alternative path
      #   separator, `File::ALT_SEPARATOR` by default. When it isn't nil,
      #   each frame's path is normalized with
      #   `Stryker::Runtime::PathKey.of`. A path with a byte beyond ASCII
      #   that Ruby tags with any encoding but UTF-8, as it does under the C
      #   locale, is tagged UTF-8 whatever `separator` is, as the paths of
      #   `paths` are. A path of only ASCII bytes is looked up as Ruby tags it,
      #   with no copy, because it finds the same key under any tag.
      def self.loading?: (Set[String] paths, ?String? separator) -> bool

      TOP_LEVEL_LABELS: Set[String]
    end
  end
end
